# First thing is getting the model backend!

The code I am going to be using is for WSL2, but if there is any Powershell, I will point it out.
First thing I noticed when I initially did this, it didn't detect the CUDA libraries of my GPU. I ran this code to install the build tools and CUDA tookkit.

'sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y git build-essential nvidia-cuda-toolkit python3 python3-pip'

The easiest way to tell it is connected to CUDA is by using this code:

'nvidia-smi' (may be different depending on your own GPU)

Next thing you would want to do is install the build dependencies.

'sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y git build-essential libopenblas-dev'
'git clone https://github.com/LostRuins/koboldcpp.git'
'make LLAMA_CUDA=1' <!--Compiles CUDA support-->

One thing I learned is since this is a WSL2 VM of Ubuntu, you can access your files directly through /mnt/c/ (handy because the model was already installed on my PC).

'python3 koboldcpp.py --model /mnt/c/AI/MN-12B-Celeste-V1.9-Q4_K_M.gguf
